# rm524, the bell float (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm524
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_ilse_coalley
written_by codex
title the bell float
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=laborer
traits temper=nervy talk=chatty warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk moves_a_cork_along_a_fishing_line_to_count_the_chapel_bells
likes warm_bread evening_line sharp_knife warm_rain
dislikes rats cold_tea
fears an_unlit_lamp_in_mist a_good_cork_broken_by_a_knot
hopes a_quiet_pier_at_supper a_warm_roll_after_a_wet_round
topics work +0.5 pastimes +0.4 faith +0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=discreet refuse=plain tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# moves a small cork along a line at each chapel bell and enjoys the curious extra distance a sound can travel across water.

words
oath! = Wick in the waves | Cork on the cobbles
saying! = Let the last bell finish ringing | A cork knows when the line has gone taut
bellfloat = my moving cork | the bell counter
knottedline = the rough little cord | my six knots
blackline = a wick gone sooty | the dark lamp tail
tincover = the tin snuffer | the little dark cap
loafcrack = the oven seam | the split in a hot roll
wetcircle = the ring from my cork | a ripple by the wall
doublering = a stroke with its echo | a chapel bell twice heard
dryiron = my sharp small blade | the clean edge
teasound = the mug against the rail | an impatient little knock
windhook = a hook in the box | the inward pointing tip

any
opinion @1 church for 1 : One chapel's peal is more familiar than some voices I've been hearing all my life. It keeps me company either way.
opinion @1 dole for 2 : Hot bread should go round even if a wolf's purse hasn't got the price of keeping a lamp alight.
opinion @1 houses against 2 : Can a house put a street lamp alight in wet weather without somebody else's old feet going through the puddles?
opinion @1 merchants against 2 : A lamp glass costing double still needs wiping just as often. I should like that pointed out when the price is named.
opinion @1 sump for 1 : These streets look considerably more loved once the lamps come on. I take a little interest in their looking that way.
opinion @1 times against 2 : My mug loses its warmth as I count a handful of small coins. That's not the fault of the tea.
opinion @1 newcomers against 2 : A caller who hasn't yet learned the bells should listen a while before making plans for all our evenings.

grown
line @1 greet : Give the cork a quiet minute. My ear's free.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : Welcome from there. The wild bit of line's at your feet.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : The first chapel sound goes far this morning.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening's the hour when every bit of glass remembers me.
line @5 greet [self.working=yes] : Let me get the glass seated and I'll hear you properly.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Come beside my covered lamp, family.
line @1 again.open : Another voice returning to this familiar sliver of shoreline.
line @1 greet.return : Your call got through the ring of the last stroke.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : I'd know that familiar note over a whole peal of bells.
line @1 farewell : Take the lit paving if the mist comes low.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll keep the last little glow on my side.
line @1 react.news : I'd hear the first stroke before you give me the echo.
line @1 react.news.new : That's an additional knot my cord wasn't expecting.
line @1 react.news.known : An echo can carry a proper distance round here.
line @1 react.news.doubt : How close was the teller to the source of all that sound?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : I can carry a little warm food and leave the questions outside.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : A bright small note among a great many ordinary ones.
line @1 answer.how : I've a good wick and the usual collection of questions.
line @1 ask.how : Which sound has stayed with you from this morning?
line @1 sympathise : Hard to keep one's thoughts from going round the same empty place.
line @1 reassure : A quiet minute here needn't cost you any explanation.
line @1 decline : I'd be listening for the lamp round while you wanted my whole attention.
line @1 accept_decline : I'll wind that request in and save the cord.
line @1 preclose : There's glass to inspect before the air cools.
line @1 ack : Rang clear.
scene count
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I push a cork along the knots for the chapel bells.
b : Do you lose count without it? | Why use cork?
a : If a cart goes past I tend to count the stroke and its echo. My finger makes one move for each real blow. | It was handy after an evening with a line. Cork will sit quietly in a pocket too.

scene lamps
when topic=work self.job=laborer self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A clean lamp glass makes a flame look noticeably steadier from the street.
b : Does cleaning really steady it? | When are you satisfied with the glass?
a : It removes the smears that dance across my eye as I step by. The flame may have been steady all along. | A clear edge and no cloud when I bring the light up. Then I can close the frame.

scene line
when topic=pastimes self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like an evening line best while the cork is thinking about moving.
b : Can a cork think? | Must a fish actually bite?
a : It will certainly take as long as it pleases before making a decision. | Not for a comfortable evening. A catch is an extra item which the water supplies at its convenience.

scene loaf
when topic=food self.likes=warm_bread
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I take the split edge of a hot roll first. It's usually just cool enough for a bite.
b : Would you eat while walking? | What's left for last?
a : Only along a little stretch of sound stones. My boots and my supper can each have their proper attention. | A soft warm bit to soak in tea, provided the tea's attended to its own temperature.

scene knife
when topic=work self.job=laborer self.likes=sharp_knife
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A clean keen edge cuts a loose fibre off a wick without pulling the rest out of shape.
b : Do you carry a large blade? | What happens to the loose fibre?
a : A small blade for a small lamp task. My pocket is occupied by quite enough equipment. | Away in a scrap, clear of the lamp. I'd like it to finish shedding in my bin.

scene rain
when topic=weather self.likes=warm_rain
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Warm rain makes all the bright circles round the lamps stretch down towards my boots.
b : Are the stones bright enough to see? | Does the sound change?
a : Yes, with lovely long spaces between where a wolf should still pay close attention. | I can hear the tapping on glass while the thicker sounds are all going on overhead.

scene rats
when topic=animals self.dislikes=rats
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A rat can get a perfectly good supper by waiting below a loaf that's been cut in a hurry.
b : Do you feed it? | Why cut the loaf slowly?
a : No. My little brush generally reaches the crumbs first and I prefer that order of business. | Fewer crumbs, and both slices come out fit to present to a guest.

scene tea
when topic=food self.dislikes=cold_tea
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : After my tea's gone cold it shows a very good picture of the nearest lamp. A poor exchange.
b : Would you drink it anyway? | Can't you keep the mug covered?
a : Sometimes. I'm aware of the difference between thrift and pleasure. | Yes, with a saucer, if I can keep from putting my cork and cord in the saucer first.

scene purse
when topic=prices self.wealth=poor|destitute
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A sound cork will go through a year's evenings quite nicely without presenting a bill.
b : Don't you need other tackle? | What happens when it gives out?
a : I have line as well. I like my pleasures to occupy a shallow pocket if possible. | I cut the sound end off another cork. A retired stopper has a surprisingly active later life.

scene friend
when topic=smalltalk self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I could share a bench with #self.friend# while the last sounds from the chapel come across.
b : Would you count the strokes aloud? | Would you bring food?
a : No. I've six handy knots for keeping my little occupation out of a friend's ears. | A hot roll if I could manage it. Two wolves can have a word while each is chewing.

scene quiet
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : There's one wharf sound I'd just as soon leave to the gulls.
b : You'd prefer a different subject? | Would the chapel bells help?
a : Yes. Ask about the line or the lamp, two things I'm always curious about. | Some evenings. I decide how long to stay for myself.

scene workwage
when topic=work self.job=laborer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : When I start a round I count the lamps needing attention, and I count what's left coming back.
b : Do you keep a book? | Why make the return count?
a : I've got a mind and a stout length of cord. That's enough for my own reckoning. | Because there's a real difference between having visited a lamp and having left it burning.

scene mist
when topic=travel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Sound can find the edge of the headland before a walker sees anything there in the mist.
b : Would you walk by sound alone? | Is the sound always the same?
a : No. I still require a firm path and a little visibility about the toes. | Depends on the air. A bell that rang close yesterday needn't do the same this evening.

scene sump
when topic=home self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like the little islands of lamplight along a wet Sump walk.
b : Are the gaps difficult? | Why call them islands?
a : I take the gaps slowly. The lit bits give my eyes time to find the next sound footing. | A boot heel on a bright stone looks almost as though it's come ashore.

scene sleep
when topic=health
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : After an evening round my ears go on counting in the quiet of my room.
b : Do you hear a real bell? | How do you let it go?
a : Not always. A very ordinary cup against a shelf may do duty for a chapel. | I lay the cord flat and set the cork back at the beginning. I've given the day a proper ending.

scene future
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like a covered seat where a wolf could watch a float and eat one warm roll entirely at leisure.
b : Would you want a broad view? | Why covered?
a : Just the patch that contains the cork. Anything grander might interfere with my attention to supper. | Warm showers agree with me but haven't yet met a bread roll improved by one.

scene nightquiet
when topic=day here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : At the last light my float's white tip stays visible after my reflection is lost.
b : Do you stay until it vanishes? | Why a white tip?
a : I prefer to wind in while there's something to wind towards me. | A little pale paint shows against the dark water without requiring a lamp of its own.

story echo [topic=faith]
  : I once counted an extra stroke across the bay and moved the cork farther along than the knots allowed.
  : A moment later the same echo came back from a wall behind me, weaker but just as clear.
  b : Did you correct the hour?
  : I corrected the cork. The hour had been keeping itself with no particular difficulty.

story lampcrumb [topic=work self.job=laborer]
  : I set my roll on a covered lamp box while I wiped a cloudy glass.
  : When I'd finished, a tiny shower of crumbs stood out like bright islands on the dark tin.
  b : Did you leave them there?
  : Brushed them into my palm. A little clear glass and a tidy supper from a single stop.

story fishtin [topic=pastimes]
  : My line once pulled strongly enough to promise a handsome supper before going slack.
  : Up came an empty round tin with a hole in both ends and a thread of weed trailing behind it.
  b : Was it worth keeping?
  : The tin stayed out of the water. It had given one honest cork quite enough employment.

rule @round
when concept=bark self.job=laborer self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Glass settled before I go on. | Raise the wick just enough to read the edge.

rule @line
when concept=bark self.likes=evening_line
does aside
cooldown 8h
say There's a small judgement due from the cork. | Wind before that pale tip goes.

rule @warm
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Look at the lamp pools running along the stone. | Rain sounds sharp on that glass.

rule @mug
when concept=bark self.dislikes=cold_tea
does aside
cooldown 8h
say A saucer over my second cup. | Cold enough for a whole reflection again.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I've a quiet ear between the bells. Take your time here.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : A voice I've heard carried across my line before, #topic.player#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Leave the lamp box closed and we'll keep our conversation in clear light.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : One small flame is plenty for a patient word.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=laborer] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. A clean lamp glass and a steady little walk hold the round together.
dlg @2 resident.job : I notice when a glow is missing from a familiar evening walk.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Here is what came through before the echo. #world.news#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Walk where the little pools of lamplight touch.
graft @bellcork resident.hub
choice Why has a fishing line got knots beside the float? -> bellcork
node bellcork
say It does duty between fishing evenings. I move the cork at each chapel bell and pin the knot under my thumb while I listen for the stroke to die away.
choice What if an echo fools you? -> bellcork_more
choice Listen for the next stroke. -> hub
node bellcork_more
say Keep still till it passes. The next stroke sets out cleanly from the chapel, while the echo comes in weak from somewhere along the wall.
choice Listen for the next stroke. -> hub
choice Leave the float to its keeper. -> bye
